<h2>What affects the price of upcoming music festivals</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The lineup.</strong> Headliners and rare reunion sets raise demand.</li>
  <li><strong>The number of days.</strong> A full weekend pass costs more than a single-day pass.</li>
  <li><strong>The access level.</strong> VIP and premium passes cost more than general admission.</li>
  <li><strong>The timing.</strong> Early availability is often broader, and prices can change closer to the date.</li>
  <li><strong>The location.</strong> Travel, lodging and local demand can shift what buyers will pay.</li>
</ul>

<div class="so-callout">
  <p><strong>Budget reminder:</strong> The pass is only part of the cost. Add travel, lodging, food, water and parking when you compare festivals, so you know your full spend.</p>
</div>

<h2>When to buy tickets for upcoming music festivals</h2>
<p>If a festival is on your must-do list, buy as soon as a pass fits your budget, because the best pass types and dates can sell down. If you are flexible, watching a listing for a few days is fine, but prices can go up as well as down. Check <a href="/ticket-deals">ticket deals</a> and our <a href="/tickets-promo-code">promo code page</a> for current offers before you pay.</p>
<p>Plan your travel to upcoming music festivals after you buy, and read the festival's own rules for re-entry, bag size and prohibited items, which are set by organizers.</p>

